{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: (1+a+b+c)/c","reason":"Since c is nonzero, the monic reciprocal-root polynomial is g(x) = x³f(1/x)/c = x³+(b/c)x²+(a/c)x+1/c. Evaluating at 1 gives g(1) = (1+a+b+c)/c.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reciprocal-root-products","label":"Use products over the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-root-products":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"evaluate-root-products","label":"Compare the root products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"evaluate-root-products":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, b² - 3a²","reason":"The third root is -2a because the roots sum to zero. Thus q = (a + bi)(a - bi) + (-2a)(2a) = a² + b² - 4a² = b² - 3a².","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"vieta-conjugate-pair","label":"Use root sums and pairwise products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"vieta-conjugate-pair":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_032"],"id":"imaginary-part-of-root-equation","label":"Compare imaginary parts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"imaginary-part-of-root-equation":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h099","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h099","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_032"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"conjugate-factor-coefficients","label":"Use the missing quadratic coefficient","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-factor-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A ((1+i√11)/2)","reason":"For a remaining root z, the quadratic has coefficients determined by 2Re(z) and |z|^2, both integers. The five squared moduli are 3, 1/2, 5/4, 5/4, and 7/2, so only A qualifies. Indeed, (x-1)(x-2)(x^2-x+3) supplies an example.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"conjugate-root-coefficients","label":"Conjugate root coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-root-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_h097"],"id":"integer-quadratic","label":"Examine the remaining quadratic","methodDirectNodeIds":{"integer-quadratic":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","cnm_h102","cnm_032","cnm_h097"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 9","reason":"For positive roots r,s,t, Vieta gives r+s+t=rst=a and rs+rt+st=b. Cauchy-Schwarz yields a²≥3b and b²≥3a², hence a≥3√3. Equality is attained only when r=s=t=√3, giving b=9 and polynomial (x-√3)³.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_096"],"id":"root-identities-and-squares","label":"Bound the coefficients using the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-identities-and-squares":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 24/5","reason":"For f(x) = x^2 + a(x-2)(x-3)(x-4), the third intersection abscissas for AB, AC, and BC are respectively 4 - 1/a, 3 - 1/a, and 2 - 1/a. Their sum gives 9 - 3/a = 24, so a = -1/5. Therefore f(0) = -24a = 24/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"secant-root-sums","label":"Use cubic root-coefficient sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-root-sums":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: -88","reason":"The roots have sum 10 and product 16. Every root is a power of 2, and these constraints force roots 1,1,2,2,2,2. Their triple-product sum is 32+48+8=88, so the coefficient B is -88.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"triple-product-sum","label":"Sum triple products of roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triple-product-sum":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h239"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h239"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-factors-expansion","label":"Determine the roots and expand","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-factors-expansion":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h240","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h240","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 208","reason":"For P, the pairwise root-product sum is 3 and the root product is 1. Thus B=(4i)^2·3=-48 and D=(4i)^4·1=256, so B+D=208.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"vieta-and-conjugation","label":"Track just two coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"vieta-and-conjugation":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"transform-the-polynomial","label":"Transform the whole polynomial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"transform-the-polynomial":["math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},"nodeIds":["math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 5 ordered pairs","reason":"The roots satisfy rst=-6. Their only absolute-value patterns are (1,1,6) and (1,2,3), allowing respectively one and four distinct root sets. These yield coefficient pairs (-6,-1), (-4,1), (-2,-5), (0,-7), and (6,11), so there are five.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"enumerate-integer-root-sets","label":"Count the possible root sets","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enumerate-integer-root-sets":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 125","reason":"Vieta gives p+q+r=-2, pq+qr+rp=-1, and pqr=-3. Hence Σp²=6 and Σp²q²=1-2(-3)(-2)=-11. Expanding the product gives 9+4(-11)+16(6)+64=125.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"symmetric-root-expressions","label":"Expand using symmetric root identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-root-expressions":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"vieta-symmetric-products","label":"Evaluate symmetric products with Vieta","methodDirectNodeIds":{"vieta-symmetric-products":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: k","reason":"Vieta's identities give a+b+c = 0, ab+bc+ca = k, and abc = -1. Grouping the expression gives a^2*b^2*(a+b) + b^2*c^2*(b+c) + c^2*a^2*(c+a). Substituting a+b = -c and its cyclic counterparts reduces this to -abc*(ab+bc+ca) = k.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduce-root-powers","label":"Use root equations and power sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-root-powers":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complete-symmetric-product","label":"Complete a symmetric product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-symmetric-product":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_151"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"The sum of the roots is the negative x² coefficient divided by the leading coefficient: -(-8)/4=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q41","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"compare-root-sum-coefficient","label":"Read the root sum from a coefficient","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-root-sum-coefficient":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1","reason":"Write the roots as m-d, m, and m+d. Their sum gives m=3/4, and their pairwise-product sum gives 3m^2-d^2=23/16, so d^2=1/4. The largest-smallest difference is 2d=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"arithmetic-roots-and-coefficients","label":"Use symmetric root identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"arithmetic-roots-and-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","cnm_220"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_h201","cnm_146","cnm_075"],"id":"symmetric-root-coefficients","label":"Use equally spaced roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-root-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","cnm_220","cnm_032","cnm_h201","cnm_146","cnm_075"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_h201","cnm_146"],"id":"middle-root-factorization","label":"Find the middle root and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"middle-root-factorization":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h203","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_224","cnm_032","cnm_h201","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 2 cos(nθ)","reason":"The quadratic x²-2cosθ·x+1=0 has roots cosθ±i sinθ. Their nth powers sum to 2cos(nθ). Equivalently, the power sums satisfy the same recurrence and initial values as 2cos(nθ).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["For 0<θ<π there is no real x satisfying the premise. The stated answer uses complex solutions, although the domain of x is not explicitly specified.","There is no real x satisfying the premise: the quadratic discriminant is −4sin²θ < 0. The stated answer assumes complex x, whose domain is not explicitly specified."],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h040","cnm_h062","cnm_h097"],"id":"symmetric-powers-and-cosine-recurrence","label":"Match the symmetric-power and cosine recurrences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-powers-and-cosine-recurrence":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_h214"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_h214","cnm_h040","cnm_h062","cnm_h097"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 78","reason":"The roots have product 2010 = 2·3·5·67 and sum a. A root divisible by 67 is either 67 or at least 134, the latter already exceeding the proposed minimum. With root 67, the other two roots have product 30 and least integer sum 11, attained by 5 and 6. Thus a_min = 67 + 5 + 6 = 78.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"large-prime-and-factor-pairs","label":"Use the large prime factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"large-prime-and-factor-pairs":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:prime-factorization","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:prime-factorization","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:extremal-principle","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3","reason":"Set t=x-2010. The boundary equation becomes g(t)=t^3-3t^2-t+1=0, with one root r1 in (-1,0), one r2 in (0,1), and one r3>1. The solution intervals are (-1,r1], (0,r2], and (1,r3]. Their total length is r1+r2+r3=3 by the cubic coefficient identity.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The zero theorem applies to the continuous cubic or separately continuous pole-free branches; keep all original rational poles excluded."],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_161","cnm_032"],"id":"solution:polynomial-sign-analysis:3ead7266f132","label":"Cubic roots and interval signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polynomial-sign-analysis":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h049","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h049","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_156","cnm_161","cnm_032"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_161","cnm_032"],"id":"solution:monotone-pole-intervals:3ead7266f132","label":"Decreasing reciprocal sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"monotone-pole-intervals":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h033","cnm_h049","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h033","cnm_h049","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_156","cnm_161","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 10","reason":"Every root r satisfies r³=5r²-8r+13. Multiplying by r^(k-2) and summing over the roots gives s_(k+1)=5s_k-8s_(k-1)+13s_(k-2). Thus (a,b,c)=(5,-8,13), and a+b+c=10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-root-equations","label":"Use the equation each root satisfies","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-root-equations":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_093"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_093"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: -88","reason":"The exponent partitions of 4 give possible root sums 21, 14, 12, 11, and 10, so the roots must be 1,1,2,2,2,2. Their triple products sum to 4·8 + 2·6·4 + 4·2 = 88. The z³ coefficient is its negative, so B = -88.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (1/32)","reason":"For theta=2k*pi/7 with k=1,2,3, the identity 1+2(cos(theta)+cos(2theta)+cos(3theta))=0 gives 8x^3+4x^2-4x-1=0 for x=cos(theta). Hence a=1/2, b=-1/2, c=-1/8, and abc=1/32.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-symmetric-sums","label":"Use cosine identities and Vieta","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-symmetric-sums":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h071","cnm_h073"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h071","cnm_h073"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (30)","reason":"Writing P(x)=10(x-r₁)(x-r₂)(x-r₃), we have P(-2)=-10(r₁+2)(r₂+2)(r₃+2). Direct substitution gives P(-2)=-80-156-58-6=-300, so the new volume is 30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. None of these; the value is 4.","reason":"Vieta gives p+q+r=1 and pq+pr+qr=1, so p²+q²+r²=-1. Each root t satisfies t³=t²-t+2. Summing gives p³+q³+r³=-1-1+6=4, which is not among A through D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148"],"id":"coefficients-and-root-equations","label":"Read the coefficients and reduce the cubes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficients-and-root-equations":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native reconciliation under the recorded task assumptions; no blind-review claim.","derivedAnswer":"E can be nonzero","reason":"Conjugate pairing makes sum b_k=0. The given equality makes sum a_k=0 too, so the full root sum is 0 and c_2003=0. The zero root gives c_0=0. Even degree with distinct roots and conjugate nonreal pairs forces another real root, making the product of b_2 through b_2004 zero. E is possible: P(x)=x^2004-2x has simple zero root and 2003 distinct nonzero roots, satisfies the root-sum condition, and has sum c_k=P(1)=-1.","recordedAnswer":"E","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h103","cnm_032"],"id":"conjugate-pairs-and-real-root-witness","label":"Use conjugate pairs and construct an example","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-pairs-and-real-root-witness":["math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h097","cnm_h103","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 244","reason":"Clearing denominators gives 1/A=(p−q)(p−r), with analogous expressions for 1/B and 1/C. Their sum is (p+q+r)²−3(pq+pr+qr). The polynomial gives p+q+r=22 and pq+pr+qr=80, so the answer is 22²−3·80=244.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"root-difference-products","label":"Sum root-difference products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-difference-products":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_161"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_148","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_161"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"derivative-at-roots","label":"Evaluate the derivative at the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"derivative-at-roots":["cnm_h217","cnm_h221","cnm_h222","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h217","cnm_h221","cnm_h222","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2","reason":"The nonzero constant term excludes zero roots. Degree 5 permits only one orbit {u, uω, uω²}, with u real. The nonreal conjugate roots have a common multiplicity k, so the multiplicities are (5-2k,k,k), giving only (3,1,1) and (1,2,2). In both cases the constant term is -u^5 = 2020, which fixes u uniquely. Both multiplicity patterns produce a valid real polynomial.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-triples-and-multiplicities","label":"Root triples and repeated roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-triples-and-multiplicities":["math:roots-of-unity","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 68","reason":"Put u=sin²(π/8) and v=cos²(π/8), so u+v=1 and uv=1/8. Grouping the complementary tangent pairs makes the expression (4/u-2)(4/v-2)=8/(uv)+4=68.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complex-powers-and-coefficients","label":"Use a polynomial for squared tangents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complex-powers-and-coefficients":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h240","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h064"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h240","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 1/105","reason":"There are 21·20 = 420 equally likely ordered pairs. Distinct integer roots with product -6 give admissible pairs (a,b) = (-6,-1), (-4,1), (-2,-5), and (0,-7). The remaining distinct-root possibility gives b = 11 and is excluded. Hence the probability is 4/420 = 1/105.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"enumerate-root-products","label":"List the possible integer roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enumerate-root-products":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_p153","cnm_h231","cnm_h137"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_p153","cnm_h231","cnm_h137"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3x⁴-bx³-1=0","reason":"Since a+b+c+d=0 and no root is zero, the new roots are -1/a, -1/b, -1/c, and -1/d. Substituting t=-1/x into t⁴-bt-3=0 gives 1+bx³-3x⁴=0, equivalent to choice D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_156","cnm_158","cnm_029"],"id":"negative-reciprocal-root-transformation","label":"Simplify the new roots to negative reciprocals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"negative-reciprocal-root-transformation":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_032","cnm_156","cnm_158","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, −256","reason":"If the roots are r,s,t, then log_2(rst)=5, so rst=32. The cubic coefficient identity gives rst=−a/8, hence a=−256.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"root-product-and-constant-term","label":"Find the product of the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-product-and-constant-term":["cnm_h044","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h043","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 250500","reason":"The integer root is 1002, so the other roots solve x²−1002x+q=0 with integer q and n=−1002q. Distinct positivity requires 1≤q≤251000. Integer pairs occur for q=k(1002−k), k=1,...,500, giving 500 exclusions. The count is 251000−500=250500.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"fixed-root-product-count","label":"Fix the integer root and count products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fixed-root-product-count":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_223","cnm_h020","cnm_h010"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_223","cnm_h020","cnm_h010","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: -a","reason":"For every root r, |r| = 1 implies 1/r = conjugate(r). Therefore the reciprocal sum is the conjugate of the root sum, which is conjugate(-a) = -a because a is real.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1987_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"],"id":"unit-circle-reciprocals","label":"Turn reciprocals into conjugates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-circle-reciprocals":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-11)","reason":"The y-intercept gives c=2, so the root product is -2. The root mean is -a/3=-2, giving a=6. Hence 1+6+b+2=-2, so b=-11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_042"],"id":"expand-root-factors","label":"Relate the zeros to the coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"expand-root-factors":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_029","cnm_211"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_029","cnm_211","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 528","reason":"For constant-term magnitudes 2,5,10,25,50, there are respectively 4,6,6,7,8 available real-root or nonreal-conjugate-pair factors. The nonunit selections number 8+6·6+4·7+4·C(6,2)=132. The factors z+1 and z²+1 are independently optional, while inclusion of z-1 is determined by the required positive constant term. Thus there are 4·132=528 polynomials.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_h097","cnm_h102","cnm_h237"],"id":"classify-root-factors","label":"Classify real-root factors and conjugate-pair factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-root-factors":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:bijective-counting","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h104","cnm_h232","cnm_h239"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:bijective-counting","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h104","cnm_h232","cnm_h239","cnm_032","cnm_h097","cnm_h102","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. The product of the non-real zeros of P","reason":"The graph shows real zeros r = 2 and 3 < s < 4, with 4 < d = P(0) < 6. Therefore the non-real pair has product d/(rs), which lies between 0 and 1. The other choices are P(-1) > 3, d > 4, P(1) > 2, and r + s > 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_196","cnm_032","cnm_029","cnm_146"],"id":"graph-bounds-and-root-product","label":"Compare quantities using graph bounds","methodDirectNodeIds":{"graph-bounds-and-root-product":["cnm_197","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_197","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums","cnm_096","cnm_082","cnm_196","cnm_032","cnm_029","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, -61","reason":"The missing cubic term makes the fourth root -6. The pairwise-product sum is a=-25, and the root product is c=-36. Thus a+c=-61.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quartic-root-identities","label":"Use all four roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quartic-root-identities":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_039","cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"linear-factors-coefficients","label":"Use the missing cubic term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"linear-factors-coefficients":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_039","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, d","reason":"Because e=0, write P(x)=xQ(x). The other four roots of Q are nonzero, so d=Q(0), their product, is nonzero. If d also vanished, zero would be a repeated root.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"vieta-product","label":"Use the nonzero-root product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"vieta-product":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h049","cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"nonzero-root-product","label":"Read the linear coefficient from the factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonzero-root-product":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h049","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. f(n)","reason":"Both bases r=(1±√5)/2 satisfy r^2=r+1. Therefore r^(n+1)-r^(n-1)=r^n, and applying this to both terms gives f(n+1)-f(n-1)=f(n).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The real-valued expression is interpreted for integer sequence indices n, since one exponential base is negative."],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduce-by-root-equations","label":"Identify the two quadratic roots and reduce the shifted powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-by-root-equations":["cnm_222","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_222","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 6 ordered triples","reason":"Set X=x, Y=2y, Z=4z. Then X+Y+Z=12, XY+YZ+ZX=44, and XYZ=48. These are the roots of t³-12t²+44t-48=(t-2)(t-4)(t-6). The three distinct roots have 3!=6 assignments, each giving a unique original triple.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"scaled-symmetric-roots","label":"Rescale to symmetric root data","methodDirectNodeIds":{"scaled-symmetric-roots":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"],"id":"scaled-symmetric-root-polynomial","label":"Rescale the variables and recover them as roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"scaled-symmetric-root-polynomial":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_146","cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, p=m³-3mn","reason":"If the original roots are r,s, then r+s=-m and rs=n. Thus -p=r³+s³=(r+s)³-3rs(r+s)=-m³+3mn, giving p=m³-3mn. Also q=n³.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduce-powers-by-root-equation","label":"Reduce powers using the root equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-powers-by-root-equation":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"reduce-root-powers","label":"Reduce cubes using the root equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-root-powers":["cnm_226","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_226","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, -3/2","reason":"Each quadratic has two nonzero real roots because its discriminant is 16+12k>0. For its roots u,v, Vieta gives 1/u+1/v=(4/k)/(-3/k)=-4/3. Thus the reciprocal sum is -2700 over 4050 roots, and the harmonic mean is 4050/(-2700)=-3/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-low-degree-coefficients","label":"Read the constant and linear coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-low-degree-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, the sum is -i, not 2","reason":"The root sum is -(-1)/i=-i. Also, the discriminant is 1-4i(2i)=9, and i(x-i)(x+2i)=0 gives roots i and -2i, confirming the other statements.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-coefficient-sum","label":"Check the root sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-coefficient-sum":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 26","reason":"Substitution gives (10+3r+s)+(24+2r)i=0. Thus r=-12 and s=26. Equivalently, the roots' product is (3+2i)(3-2i)=13=s/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"conjugate-root-product","label":"Use the conjugate root product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-root-product":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 0","reason":"Substitution gives 2h+k=16 and h-k=2, hence h=6 and k=4. Therefore |2h-3k|=|12-12|=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-missing-quadratic-coefficient","label":"Use the missing x-squared term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-missing-quadratic-coefficient":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B","reason":"For k<0, substituting z=iy gives 10y²-3y+k=0. Its discriminant is 9-40k>0, and its roots are real and nonzero. Multiplying them by i gives both roots of the original equation, so both are pure imaginary.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-sum-product-signs","label":"Use root sum and product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-sum-product-signs":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 194","reason":"Squaring a+a^-1 = 4 gives a^2+a^-2 = 16-2 = 14. Squaring again gives a^4+a^-4 = 14^2-2 = 194.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"power-sum-recurrence","label":"Use a quadratic-root recurrence","methodDirectNodeIds":{"power-sum-recurrence":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: -7007","reason":"Write f(x)=(x+t)g(x). Comparing x coefficients gives 10+t=100, so t=90; comparing x³ coefficients gives a+t=1, so a=-89. Consequently g(1)=1-89+1+10=-77 and f(1)=91(-77)=-7007.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"extra-root-vieta","label":"Root-coefficient identities for the extra root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extra-root-vieta":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -7007","reason":"Write f(x)=(x+t)g(x). Comparing coefficients of x gives 10+t=100, so t=90. Comparing coefficients of x^3 gives a+t=1, so a=-89. Therefore f(1)=91(1-89+1+10)=-7007.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shared-root-vieta","label":"Vieta with the fourth root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shared-root-vieta":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 440","reason":"We have x²+y²=4²-2(-2)=20 and x³+y³=4³-3(-2)(4)=88. The expression equals (x²+y²)(x³+y³)/(xy)², s