Ids":{"conjugate-roots-and-evaluation":["math:rational-irrational-operation-proofs","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-irrational-operation-proofs","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-coefficient-sums"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 9","reason":"Let α=15+√220, β=15-√220, and S_n=α^n+β^n. Then S_n=30S_(n-1)-5S_(n-2), with S_1=30 and S_2=890, so every S_n for n≥1 is an integer divisible by 10. Since 0<β^19+β^82<1, the requested number has integer part S_19+S_82-1, ending in 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"conjugate-power-recurrence","label":"Track integer conjugate power sums","nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_147","cnm_032","cnm_079","cnm_p153"],"id":"solution:conjugate-power-recurrence:c6edb0b208bf","label":"Build a recurrence for the conjugate power sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-power-recurrence":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_h214","math:modular-congruence","math:floor-ceiling"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h200","cnm_h214","math:modular-congruence","math:floor-ceiling","cnm_147","cnm_032","cnm_079","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 4","reason":"Native mathematical derivation agrees with the recorded choice; no grading change proposed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"local-factor-sign-and-degree","label":"Local factor sign, degree counting and coefficient comparison","nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:elementary-function-limits","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stationary-contact-and-degree","label":"Stationary polynomial zeros and repeated factor division","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stationary-contact-and-degree":["cnm_h226","cnm_h217","cnm_h221","cnm_h222","math:elementary-function-limits","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h226","cnm_h217","cnm_h221","cnm_h222","math:elementary-function-limits","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, -100","reason":"The quartic root sums give linear coefficients 38 for Q and 108 for P. Write Q=(x+19)^2+k. Its values at the first four roots are k+4 and k+16, whose sum must be -108, so k=-64. These are P's roots -60 and -48, giving P=(x+54)^2-36. The minima sum to -64-36=-100; these forms also produce the stated roots of Q(P(x)).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"composition-coefficients","label":"Compare quartic root sums","nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_226","cnm_232"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"solution:composition-coefficients:da8031b8c9dc","label":"Compare coefficients of the compositions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"composition-coefficients":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_233","cnm_235"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_233","cnm_235","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 6","reason":"Set w = z+2i. The polynomial becomes w^3+w+10 = (w+2)(w^2-2w+5), with roots -2 and 1+/-2i. Thus the original roots are -2-2i, 1, and 1-4i. The last two form a vertical base of length 4, and the first has horizontal distance 3 from that base. The area is 4*3/2 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"center-shift-and-vieta","label":"Translate and use root identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-shift-and-vieta":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, -6","reason":"The roots are 3-i and -2+i: their sum is 1 and their product is -5+5i, as required by z²-z-5+5i=0. Their real parts multiply to 3·(-2)=-6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_032","cnm_148"],"id":"symmetric-real-part-product","label":"Use the sum and product of the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-real-part-product":["cnm_h099","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h099","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h102","cnm_224","cnm_h097","cnm_032","cnm_148"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","status":"mapped","total":53}
